findings.evidence:
SymbolTable.cpp:265-269 `if (token_is_in_global_scope(token_to_update, scope_id)) { token_to_update = get_globabl_token(token_to_update); token_to_update.scope_id = scope_id; }`. Instrumented run for `program test is
global variable myglobal : integer;
begin
    myglobal := 5;
end program.
` printed `DEBUG global myglobal identifer_type (0=NONE,1=PROC,2=VAR,3=TYPE,4=PROGNAME) = 0`. Instrumented run for `global procedure myproc : integer () variable q : integer; begin q := 1; end procedure; begin end program.` printed `DEBUG global myproc identifer_type = 0`. Instrumented run for `global variable arr : integer[10]; begin arr[2] := 5; end program.` printed `DEBUG global arr is_array = 0`, while an equivalent local (non-global) `variable localarr : integer[10]; ... localarr[2] := 5;` in scope 0 printed `DEBUG scope0 localarr is_array = 1`.

SymbolTable.cpp:349-355 `if (token_is_in_global_scope(test_token, scope_id)) { token_to_update = get_globabl_token(test_token); token_to_update.scope_id = scope_id; token_to_update.identifier_data_type = data_type; token_to_update.global_scope = true; }` followed by `resync_tables(token_to_update.scope_id, token_to_update);` at SymbolTable.cpp:373 (which re-writes scope_table[-1] because global_scope is true). Instrumented test `program test is
global variable x : float;
procedure foo : integer ()
    variable x : integer;
begin
    x := 5;
    return 0;
end procedure;
begin
    x := 314;
end program.
` printed `DEBUG global x data_type (0=NONE,1=INT,2=FLOAT,3=STRING,4=BOOL) = 1`; the same program with the shadowing declaration removed (`variable y : integer;` instead) printed `= 2` (FLOAT, correct). Spec text: spec2023.txt lines ~127-131 'Global names must be unique. Local names ... can also shadow (or overload) a global name.'

parser.cpp:466-469 (procedure header), 1255-1258, 1335-1338 (both variable-declaration overloads), 1434-1437 (type declaration) all contain the identical pattern: `if (!Lexer->symbol_table.is_global_token(Current_parse_token)) { Lexer->symbol_table.make_token_global(Current_parse_token); } else { }`. Test `program test is
global variable x : integer;
global variable x : float;
begin
    x := 314;
end program.
` produced `The program parsed successfully with no errors` and DEBUG output showing x's final type as FLOAT(2), i.e. the redeclaration silently overwrote the first without any diagnostic. Spec text: spec2023.txt line ~127 'Global names must be unique.'
